    sources: [{ id: "src_vespucci_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Amerigo Vespucci (Q47674), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-24): English label 'Amerigo Vespucci', description 'Florentine explorer, financier, navigator and cartographer', Korean label 아메리고 베스푸치, instance of human, born 1454-03-09, died 1512-02-22, citizenship Republic of Florence and Crown of Castile. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q47674" }, { id: "src_vespucci_dbi", kind: "biography", title: "Luciano Formisano, 'Vespucci, Amerigo', Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani vol. 99 (Treccani, 2020; read in full in Italian up to its bibliography). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: born in Florence 9 March 1454; educated by his uncle Giorgio Antonio; secretary on the 1478 Paris embassy; steward and factotum of Lorenzo di Pierfrancesco de' Medici (seventy-one letters to him, 1483-91); in Seville from 1491-92 as factor in Giannotto Berardi's firm, which outfitted ships for Columbus; the firm collapsed after Berardi died in December 1495 and the first four caravels wrecked in February 1496, leaving Vespucci to liquidate it, unemployed at forty-two; voyage of 1499-1500 as pilot (astronomer and cartographer, never a ship's commander) with Ojeda and de la Cosa; Portuguese service and the 1501-02 voyage; summoned to the Spanish court in 1505; naturalised in April 1505; named piloto mayor of the Casa de la Contratacion in March 1508 with the duty to update the official chart and examine pilots; asked in 1509 for opinions on exports and on a signature of Columbus; will of April 1511; died 22 February 1512. Detailed account of the contested printed letters (Mundus novus and the Soderini letter), of Magnaghi's forgery thesis and of its philological refutation: the DBI concludes the printed letters are not purely his and that his role does not depend on them. Authoritative scholarly reference entry.", url: "https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/amerigo-vespucci_(Dizionario-Biografico)/" }, { id: "src_vespucci_britannica", kind: "biography", title: "Roberto Almagia, 'Amerigo Vespucci', Encyclopaedia Britannica (read in full): son of a notary, humanist education from his uncle; 1479 trip with a relative sent to the French king by the Medici; Medici 'bank' and Seville agency under Berardi; two series of documents on his voyages (printed letters and private letters to the Medici) and Magnaghi's theory that the printed series are manipulations; the 1499-1500 voyage under Ojeda as navigator 'certainly authentic'; Spain did not welcome his proposals and he entered Portuguese service at the end of 1500; the 1501-02 voyage after which he and scholars became convinced the lands were a 'New World'; Waldseemuller's 1507 proposal of the name America; summoned to Spain in 1505 and made chief navigator of the Casa de Contratacion in 1508 to examine pilots and prepare the official map; scholars' charge that he usurped others' merits, answered with 'a genuine pioneer of Atlantic exploration'. Reference article by a geographer; sympathetic but flags the controversy.", url: "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Amerigo-Vespucci" }, { id: "src_vespucci_ewb", kind: "biography", title: "'Amerigo Vespucci', Encyclopedia of World Biography (Gale, reproduced on Encyclopedia.com; read in full): Florentine navigator and pilot major of Castile; partnership with Berardi until his death at the end of 1495; Columbus's reaction to his 1496 conversations; doubts about the Soderini letter's authenticity and the 'first voyage' (Magnaghi, Pohl against, Arciniegas for); the 1499 voyage with Ojeda documented; the Portuguese voyage; Columbus's 1505 letter to his son calling him a man of good will to whom fortune had been unkind; Waldseemuller's naming, to which Vespucci 'was no party'; first holder of the pilot major office from 1508, training and examining pilots and keeping the Padron Real. Short general-reference entry; notes that biographers differ sharply.", url: "https://www.encyclopedia.com/people/history/explorers-travelers-and-conquerors-biographies/amerigo-vespucci" }, { id: "src_vespucci_catholic", kind: "biography", title: "Gustavo Uzielli, 'Amerigo Vespucci', The Catholic Encyclopedia vol. 15 (New York: Robert Appleton Company, 1912; read in full on New Advent). Early reference biography by Vespucci's Florentine editor and a defender; dates his birth 1451 (the modern standard is 1454) and accepts all four voyages as narrated in the Soderini letter, which later critical scholarship questions; useful for the archival material it cites: letters to the Medici, the 1478-80 Paris embassy reports, the 1505 naturalisation decree and the 1508 appointment as piloto mayor, his marriage to Maria Cerezo and her 1512 pension.", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/15384b.htm" }, { id: "src_vespucci_omodeo", kind: "institution", title: "Pietro Omodeo, 'The Final Years of Vespucci and His Scientific Merits' (chapter 14 of 'Amerigo Vespucci: The Historical Context of His Explorations and Scientific Contribution', Knowledge Hegemonies series, Edizioni Ca' Foscari, Venice University Press; read in full, 18 pages). PARTISAN DEFENCE: Vespucci made piloto mayor in 1508, taught navigation and map-making and a longitude method at home, was made to swear to give copies of the official chart only to authorised persons, gave his opinion on commerce, left a 1511 will (revoked) with Rondinelli as friend and executor and long unpaid loans owed to him; argues the usurpation charge descends from Pluche, Charlevoix, Munoz, Navarrete and Emerson without documentation; claims a Portuguese damnatio memoriae. Written by a Vespucci sympathiser; used only for facts the DBI also records and flagged as partisan wherever it goes further.", url: "https://edizionicafoscari.it/media/pdf/books/978-88-6969-403-5/978-88-6969-403-5-ch-16_DGp7mwP.pdf" }, { id: "src_vespucci_mariners", kind: "institution", title: "'Amerigo Vespucci', Ages of Exploration, The Mariners' Museum and Park (read in full): a popular museum biography with no named author; Vespucci's education and Medici work, Seville contacts through Berardi, the king's request that he sail as pilot (cartography and astronomy) in 1499, his southern-sky observations, the Portuguese voyages, a longitude method, the pilot-major role (training and licensing pilots, managing maps), Waldseemuller's naming and his death from malaria-weakened health in 1512. General-audience page; repeats the standard narrative including the printed letters.", url: "https://exploration.marinersmuseum.org/subject/amerigo-vespucci/" }],
