    sources: [{ id: "src_hikmet_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Nâzım Hikmet (Q184906), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-24): English label 'Nâzım Hikmet', description 'Turkish communist poet, playwright and novelist (1902-1963)', Korean label 나짐 히크메트, instance of human, born 15 January 1902 (an alternate value of 30 November 1901 is also recorded), died 3 June 1963, citizenship recorded as Ottoman Empire, Turkey and Poland. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q184906" }, { id: "src_hikmet_wikipedia", kind: "wikipedia", title: "Wikipedia, 'Nâzım Hikmet' (raw text opened for orientation on dates and the birth-date discrepancy). Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N%C3%A2z%C4%B1m_Hikmet" }, { id: "src_hikmet_gale", kind: "biography", title: "Gale biographical entry 'Hikmet, Nazim' reproduced on Encyclopedia.com (read in full). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: calls him posthumously one of the giants of twentieth-century Turkish literature, whose poems, plays and prose were banned in Turkey for most of his life; says he revolutionized Turkish verse by challenging traditional forms with broken lines and a street-vernacular style, and was the first Turkish poet to win an international reputation; sentenced in 1938 and held until 1950, with sources contradicting each other on the charge and on how his prison time ended; writes that his most ambitious project, the five-volume Human Landscapes of nearly twenty thousand lines, was written in prison between 1938 and 1950 and published only after his death; quotes critics (Halman, Andrews, Konuk) and his own remark that the artist is 'the engineer of the human soul'. Reference entry that leans on critics' judgments; admiring, and openly notes contradictory accounts.", url: "https://encyclopedia.com/arts/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/hikmet-nazim" }, { id: "src_hikmet_salem", kind: "biography", title: "EBSCO Research Starters, 'Nazim Hikmet' (a Salem Press literary biography; read in full): imprisonment of seventeen years in total; the 1938 arraignment for inciting unrest in the armed forces after cadets were found with his poems; the epic Human Landscapes begun in Bursa prison and circulated in parts among friends and family, with some portions confiscated; a 1949 hunger strike of seventeen days after a heart attack; release in 1950, then a conscription order, and flight alone in a small fishing boat to a Romanian ship in 1951; work as proofreader, translator and scriptwriter and publication of columns under a pseudonym; plays that experimented with old and new forms and that drew criticism for facile identification of characters with political positions; a play of the 1950s that took issue with the personality cult and rigid criticism of the Stalin era, yet the author is also said to have continued to uphold Soviet positions on international security, with some late works doing him little credit. Scholarly reference biography with critical remarks.", url: "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/literature-and-writing/nazim-hikmet" }, { id: "src_hikmet_poetryfoundation", kind: "institution", title: "Poetry Foundation, 'Nazim Hikmet' biography page (read): left Turkey after the First World War to study in Moscow, became a Marxist; arrested after returning in 1924, escaped to Russia, came back in 1928; in the next ten years wrote many works including the 1936 Epic of Shaykh Bedreddin and the 20,000-line Human Landscapes; shifted from syllabic patriotic verse to plainer language and Turkish folk traditions; left in 1951 after long imprisonment; his works were banned in Turkey until 1965. Short institutional biography.", url: "https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/nazim-hikmet" }, { id: "src_hikmet_britannica", kind: "biography", title: "Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Nazim Hikmet' (read): calls him one of the most important and influential figures in twentieth-century Turkish literature; briefly attended the naval academy, studied in Moscow, returned a Marxist in 1924; his mastery of language and introduction of free verse and a wide range of themes influenced Turkish literature in the late 1930s; influenced by the Russian Futurists he tried to 'depoetize' poetry, later with a quieter style; the Epic of Shaykh Bedreddin (1936) and the 20,000-line Portraits of People from My Land; also plays in vigorous prose. Short reference article.", url: "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Nazim-Hikmet" }, { id: "src_hikmet_hurriyet", kind: "press", title: "Hurriyet Daily News, 'Poems of freedom from behind iron bars, exile' (7 January 2009, on the restoration of his citizenship; read in full): chronology of the 1925 conviction after which he fled, a 1928 return and detention, his 1931 trial in which he is quoted answering that he was a communist poet and was found not guilty, the 1933 conviction, the 1938 arrest and sentences combined to 28 years and 4 months, the newspaper campaign of 1949, several hunger strikes and hospitalizations, release in 1950 after 12 years and 7 months, and the report that poems written in prison were smuggled abroad. Turkish newspaper summary.", url: "https://hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/poems-of-freedom-from-behind-iron-bars-exile-10713439" }, { id: "src_hikmet_sabah", kind: "press", title: "Daily Sabah (Hakan Arslanbenzer), 'Nazim Hikmet: Human scenes from my homeland' (17 October 2015; read in full): early life, the 1925 court sentence and flight, a 1928 attempt to cross the border illegally that ended in several months of detention, the 1938 life sentence in the Navy Case, flight to Romania in 1951 and loss of citizenship; argues his poetry worked as a propaganda tool for the Turkish left for decades, yet says critics of every political view accept him as a major poet for his fluent, natural Turkish, and that Human Landscapes draws its power from portraits of Anatolian villagers he met in prison. Opinion feature that is openly critical of the poet's use of verse as propaganda while praising his language.", url: "https://www.dailysabah.com/portrait/2015/10/17/nazim-hikmet-human-scenes-from-my-homeland" }, { id: "src_hikmet_ktb", kind: "institution", title: "Republic of Turkiye Ministry of Culture and Tourism, biography pages 'Nazim Hikmet' (two pages read): navy school and discharge for ill health, the 1921 move to Anatolia and then Moscow, the 1925 verdict in absentia and flight, the 1928 detention at Hopa, newspaper and film-studio work and the use of the pen name Orhan Selim, the 1938 sentences reduced to 28 years and four months, a hunger strike that came with a campaign for amnesty, release in 1950 'after 13 years of imprisonment' (the second page says about 12 years), flight in 1951 on a Romanian vessel via a motorboat, publication of poems under pseudonyms in the 1940s, list of works across poetry, plays, novels, letters and fairy tales, and the 2002 'Year of Nazim'. State-run biography page; its figures differ slightly from other sources.", url: "https://www.ktb.gov.tr/EN-118010/nazim-hikmet.html" }],
