    sources: [{ id: "src_qian_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Qian Xuesen (Q333500), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-24): English label 'Qian Xuesen', description 'Chinese rocket scientist (1911-2009)', instance of human, born 11 December 1911, died 31 October 2009, citizenship People's Republic of China, Korean label and Korean Wikipedia title 첸쉐썬, aliases include Hsue-Shen Tsien and the Chinese form 錢學森. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q333500" }, { id: "src_qian_britannica", kind: "biography", title: "Gregory Kulacki, 'Qian Xuesen', Encyclopaedia Britannica (read in full). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: engineer and research scientist widely called the 'father of Chinese aerospace' for his role in establishing China's ballistic missile program; studied at MIT and Caltech under Theodore von Karman and became an expert in aerodynamics and jet propulsion; helped analyse the German rocket program and debriefed German scientists in 1945; detained in 1950 and kept under house arrest for five years before returning to China in 1955; director of the Fifth Academy of the Ministry of National Defense from 1956 and of the Chinese Academy of Space Technology from 1968. The entry stresses that he was not directly responsible for any specific missile, rocket engine or satellite and earned his title by training China's first generation of aerospace engineers. Reference biography by a scholar of Chinese space policy; sympathetic but corrective of the legend.", url: "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Qian-Xuesen" }, { id: "src_qian_lv", kind: "biography", title: "Chengdong Lv, 'Return to China One Day: The Learning Life of Qian Xuesen' (Springer and Zhejiang Science and Technology Publishing House, 2023, open access; translated by Yingchun Zhang; chapters 2 to 4 and 6 read, covering his schooling, his musical and design interests, his Caltech and MIT years and his 1950 to 1955 detention and return; the author is at the Qian Xuesen Library of Shanghai Jiao Tong University). Admiring institutional biography built on archival documents, letters of recommendation by von Karman, diaries and student recollections (including a former MIT student's account of his lectures); the account of the 1950 case is told from his side.", url: "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-19-5755-0" }, { id: "src_qian_galcit", kind: "institution", title: "Caltech Division of Engineering and Applied Science, 'Qian Xuesen (Tsien Hsue-Shen)', Legends of GALCIT (read in full): aerodynamicist and cyberneticist recruited from MIT to von Karman's group at Caltech; involved in the rocket experiments of the founding team of the Jet Propulsion Laboratory and, in 1943, with two colleagues drafted the first document to use the name 'Jet Propulsion Laboratory'; Robert H. Goddard Professor of Jet Propulsion from 1949; five years of surveillance during which he published 'Engineering Cybernetics' (1954), reviewed at once as a major contribution to control theory; his travel ban lifted on 4 August 1955; return to China and his role in its missile and space program. Department history page; admiring of its alumnus, but frank about the security case.", url: "https://aerospace.caltech.edu/about/legends-of-galcit/qian-xuesen-tsien-hsue-shen" }, { id: "src_qian_archives", kind: "institution", title: "Caltech Archives and Special Collections, 'Death of JPL co-founder' (Caltech Library blog, 1 December 2009; read in full): says he came to the United States in 1935, was a key figure in the transition from propeller aircraft to jet engines and rockets, collaborated with von Karman and Frank Malina in founding the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, was invited in 1948 to be the Goddard Professor and head of the Guggenheim Jet Propulsion Center, lost his security clearance in 1950 over alleged Communist associations despite Lee DuBridge's defence, and decided himself in 1955 to return to China, where he led its space and military rocketry programs. Archive blog written for his obituary; same institution as the department page above.", url: "https://library.caltech.edu/blog/Death-of-JPL-co-founder" }, { id: "src_qian_lindahall", kind: "institution", title: "William B. Ashworth Jr., 'Hsue-Shen Tsien', Scientist of the Day, Linda Hall Library (31 October 2023; read in full): historian's short account that he studied at MIT and Caltech under von Karman, was among the founders of the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, went with von Karman in 1945 to interview German rocket experts including Wernher von Braun, was stripped of his clearance in 1950 and effectively under house arrest for five years while still allowed to teach, left in 1955 in an exchange of prisoners, and led the Chinese rocket, nuclear-weapons and space programs; also calls him one of the founders of systems engineering. Short, admiring and critical of the US treatment of him; the systems engineering claim is not repeated by the other sources.", url: "https://www.lindahall.org/about/news/scientist-of-the-day/hsue-shen-tsien/" }, { id: "src_qian_rfa", kind: "press", title: "Radio Free Asia, \"'Father' of China's Nuke Dies\" (obituary and commentary, 2 November 2009; read in full): reports that he was credited with leading China's first missile project and its first satellite, that in 1958 he published articles claiming China's crop harvest could reach as much as 5,000 kilos per mu and that this assessment fed the disastrous agricultural policy of that year, with commentators disagreeing whether it was his own error or the product of political pressure, and that he later supported the study of qigong. Independent news source and a counter-voice to the state obituary; the commentary is opinion and is not used for any trait.", url: "https://www.rfa.org/english/news/china/scientistdead-11022009180056.html" }],
