      impact_motivation: [68, 0.52, "s", "A"],
      // [src_rh_memoir, src_rh_rhoads, src_rh_railton | 1850s | memoir, scholarly history, interpretive essay] He turned down church backers who wanted a promise that he would become a missionary and, back in China, turned down a comprador's post as servile, preferring to travel as the firm's agent or to translate independently (his memoir; Rhoads and Railton repeat it). Both episodes are his own account, restated rather than independently documented, and Rhoads says he found suitable work with difficulty at first. CONCENTRATION CHECK (agent): lowered from 66 / 0.52 strong inference to 58 / 0.44 inference because every instance traces to the memoir; impact is dual-edged because the refusals left him struggling for work at first.
      autonomy_need: [58, 0.44, "i", "D"],
      // [src_rh_rhoads, src_rh_memoir | 1868-1871 | scholarly history, memoir] Rhoads credits the Mission's approval to the combination of his lobbying with the patronage of Zeng Guofan and Li Hongzhang, and relates that he won over Ding Richang, who then moved Zeng; but Zeng's memorial credits Ding rather than Yung with the idea, and a Chinese historian doubts his account of the petition. CONCENTRATION CHECK (agent): lowered from 62 / 0.5 strong inference to 58 / 0.46 inference because the chain of persuasion is told from his own account and the credit is contested.
      persuasiveness: [58, 0.46, "i", "A"],
      // [src_rh_rhoads | 1871-1881 | scholarly history] When the scholar-official class would not enrol its sons, Yung and the Shanghai merchant Xu Run used kinship and native-place ties to find candidates, and Yung went to his home village to ask relatives and friends; in 1881, with Leonard Bacon, he helped two recalled students, Tan Yaoxun and Rong Kui, find independent support so that they could stay in the United States. Two documented episodes, both from one scholar. CONCENTRATION CHECK (agent): lowered from 68 / 0.56 to 64 / 0.52 because both come from a single scholar. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered to 62 / 0.5 because credit is shared with Xu Run and Bacon.
      resourcefulness: [62, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_rh_rhoads, src_rh_memoir | 1870 | scholarly history, memoir] Rhoads says that when Governor Ding Richang took him north after the Tianjin massacre, Yung capitalised on the unexpected chance to remind him of the educational scheme, which Zeng Guofan then backed; Rhoads also credits Li Hongzhang, not Yung, with the argument from the Burlingame Treaty's education clause. A single episode. CONCENTRATION CHECK (agent): lowered from 62 / 0.48 to 60 / 0.44 because it is one episode told from one chain of accounts.
